WEBVTT

cue-VID_U6_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Meet Northstar Microchip Imports, a British firm buying sensors from abroad. A glowing network links markets. Lines thicken as containers and data cross borders. This is globalisation: worldwide trade and the movement of people and capital between countries. Northstar sits on one small node, but the whole web moves it.

cue-VID_U6_03-2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Globalisation opens markets. Improved transport, faster communication and free trade agreements let Northstar reach buyers in six countries. Brand awareness spreads online. But threats travel with the same links: overseas rivals undercut prices, and a shock in one country travels along every line. The network creates opportunity and vulnerability together. A supplier strike overseas can stop the assembly line here, so managers watch the whole map.

cue-VID_U6_03-3
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Now watch Vantara Devices, a multinational with operations in more than one country. Its parent sends two million dollars of capital to Vantara Assembly Works. The subsidiary creates one hundred fifty jobs and earns five million dollars of exports. Six hundred thousand dollars of profit flows back to the parent. Host countries gain investment, jobs and choice, but face competition and profit repatriation and pressure on local firms.

cue-VID_U6_03-4
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Exchange rates state one currency in terms of another. Here one dollar buys seventy five pence. A British buyer paying four hundred dollars for sensors needs three hundred pounds, because four hundred times nought point seven five equals three hundred. Reverse it: a three hundred pound order costs an American buyer four hundred dollars. Cards animate both directions. Every purchase crosses the rate, so every price changes twice.

cue-VID_U6_03-5
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Now the dollar depreciates: one dollar buys sixty pence, so the pound appreciates. The same three hundred pound order now costs the American buyer five hundred dollars, because three hundred divided by nought point six is five hundred. British exports are dearer. Meanwhile a four hundred dollar American order costs a British buyer two hundred forty pounds, not three hundred, so imports are cheaper. Northstar gains on costs, but its sales slow.

cue-VID_U6_03-6
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:40.000
A tariff is a tax put on imported goods entering the country. Here a border gate adds a ten percent tariff to a four hundred dollar component. That is forty dollars, so the landed cost becomes four hundred forty dollars. At one dollar to seventy five pence, Northstar pays three hundred thirty pounds instead of three hundred. The gate closes on cheap inputs and opens for local rivals.

cue-VID_U6_03-7
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:08.000
A quota is a limit on the physical quantity of a product that can be imported. At the border gate, twelve thousand sensors are wanted but only eight thousand pass. The four thousand unit gap pushes the price up from three hundred to three hundred thirty pounds. Domestic producers sell more, while Northstar faces higher costs and less choice. It may switch to local suppliers, or pass the increase to customers.

cue-VID_U6_03-8
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Free trade and protection each have two sides. Free trade gives Northstar cheaper inputs, wider markets and more choice. Because competition is strong, therefore prices fall, so consumers benefit. Yet domestic firms can be squeezed and jobs lost. Protection saves jobs and infant industries, but tariffs raise costs, quotas limit supply and other countries may retaliate. For an importing firm, the balance tilts toward free trade. If the pound appreciates, imports get cheaper anyway, so a tariff hurts less. If the dollar strengthens, protection may look kinder.

cue-VID_U6_03-9
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Now apply it. One dollar buys seventy five pence. A British firm owes four hundred dollars to a supplier. Later the dollar depreciates to sixty pence. What does the bill become in pounds, and are its exports dearer or cheaper for American buyers? On screen, two currency cards flip and a border gate opens. Choose, then watch the arrows settle. Two hundred forty pounds is the new bill, so imports are cheaper. A three hundred pound export now costs five hundred dollars, so overseas buyers pay more.

